  <h1>About whistleblowerawardcalc.com</h1>
  <p>whistleblowerawardcalc.com is a free whistleblower-award resource maintained by The Click Lab. Our calculator and guides help people who suspect fraud against the government, the SEC, the CFTC, or the IRS understand what a monetary award could realistically look like under the SEC, CFTC, IRS, and False Claims Act whistleblower programs &mdash; before they've spoken with an attorney or filed anything. The Site is built for people early in the process, often before they've decided whether to report at all, who want a plain-English starting point &mdash; not a substitute for legal advice.</p>

  <h2>What we do</h2>
  <p>We translate four federal whistleblower programs &mdash; the SEC Whistleblower Program, the CFTC Whistleblower Program, the IRS Whistleblower Program, and False Claims Act qui tam suits &mdash; into plain-English calculators and guides. Pages are checked against the <a href="/editorial/">editorial standards</a> and cite the governing statutes, agency rules, and published award orders. The calculator estimates a potential award range based on the program, the total sanctions or recovery involved, and the quality and consistency of a whistleblower's contribution and cooperation &mdash; the factors agencies themselves say move an award up or down within the statutory range.</p>

  <h2>Who this calculator is for</h2>
  <p>Most visitors fall into one of a few groups: employees or industry insiders who've noticed something they believe is fraud and want to understand the financial stakes before deciding whether, or how, to report it; people who have already filed a tip or a qui tam complaint and want context on how the award process typically unfolds; and attorneys, paralegals, or compliance professionals who want a quick reference point before pulling the specific statute and agency rule that applies to a given case. We built the Site around these use cases, which is why every guide leads with a plain-English answer before it gets into the statutory detail.</p>

  <h2>How the estimate is built</h2>
  <p>The full mechanics &mdash; the statutory award ranges for each program, the minimum-sanctions thresholds, and the contribution and cooperation adjustments &mdash; are documented on our <a href="/methodology/">methodology page</a>. At a high level: the calculator applies each program's mandatory award percentage range to the sanctions or recovery you enter, checks whether the case clears that program's minimum threshold, then adjusts within the range based on how strong and how cooperative your reported contribution was. Naming the actual violation, evaluating the strength of the evidence, and navigating the confidential filing procedure each program requires is work only an attorney experienced in that specific program can do.</p>

  <h2>What we don't do</h2>
  <p>We don't provide legal advice, and nothing on this Site creates an attorney-client relationship. Actual whistleblower awards depend on facts a calculator can't see: the specificity and originality of the information, whether it actually triggered or advanced an investigation, the agency's discretion, and whether other whistleblowers reported the same conduct. We don't evaluate individual cases, and we don't review or file tips or qui tam complaints. The guides don't recommend particular attorneys or law firms, and the Site doesn't collect a case description to send to one. For the offices that actually take filings, see <a href="/#where-to-get-help">Where to get help</a> on the calculator page &mdash; the SEC, CFTC, IRS, Department of Justice, and FinCEN &mdash; and our <a href="/contact/">contact page</a> for how a state bar referral service fits in. The <a href="/disclaimer/">disclaimer</a> states the limits of an estimate.</p>

  <h2>Editorial standards</h2>
  <p>Every guide and reference page is written from primary sources &mdash; the governing statutes, agency rules, and published award determinations from the SEC, CFTC, IRS, and Department of Justice &mdash; and reviewed before publication. Each page is meant to answer a specific question a prospective whistleblower would actually ask, with enough detail to be useful when the decision to report is genuinely high-stakes. The sourcing rules are on the <a href="/editorial/">editorial standards</a> page. Where a page names a statute or an office, the name links to the text or the agency.</p>

  <h2>How we make money</h2>
  <p>Display advertising through Google AdSense. No law firm or whistleblower-representation service pays for a mention, a placement in the guides, or a referral. The Site does not sell case information. An advertisement that happens to be for a law firm is Google's placement, not our recommendation. The <a href="/privacy/">privacy policy</a> describes the ad and analytics tags. The <a href="/terms/">terms</a> cover acceptable use of the calculator.</p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>Questions, corrections, or feedback: see our <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.</p>
</BaseLayout>
